Wearable electrochemical biosensors for perspiration analysis offer a promising non-invasive biomarker moni­ toring method. Herein, a functionalized hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC)-encapsulated flexible 3D porous graphene-based epidermal patch was firstly fabricated for monitoring sweat glucose, lactate, $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ and temper­ ature using simple, cost-effective, laser-engraved, and spray-coating techniques. The fabricated H-NPC-modified electrode significantly increased electrochemical surface area and electrocatalytic activity. Within the physio­ logical sweat range $\left(0{-}1.5~\mathrm{mM}\right)$ , the second-generation glucose sensor exhibited an excellent sensitivity of 82.7 $\upmu\mathrm{AmM}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with $0.025~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ LOD. Moreover, the lactate biosensor exhibited an extraordinary linear range $\left(0{-}56\mathrm{mM}\right)$ response owing to the incorporation of an outer diffusion limiting layer (DLL) that controls the lactate flux reaching the enzyme with comparable sensitivity $(204\mathrm{\nAmM^{-1}c m^{-2}})$ and LOD $(4~\upmu\up M)$ . Finally, we employed an analytical correction approach incorporating pH and temperature adjustments during on-body tests. In addition to connecting various carbon-based materials to limitless metal-organic frameworks as a transduction material, our research also paves the way for enabling these sensors to operate on $\mathtt{p H}$ and T correction independently while delivering accurate results.
## 1. Introduction
Wearable electrochemical biosensors based on sweat analysis offer a feasible method to monitor biomarkers without invasive testing(Imani et al., 2016). Electrochemical biosensing is the most extensively used method for sweat glucose and lactate detection, which has low sensi­ tivity, and a linear range of lactate detection well below expected levels in sweat(Xuan et al., 2021). The expected range of sweat glucose is $10{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ while sweat lactate is considered to be within $3.7{-}50~\mathrm{mM}$ (Wang et al., 2021; Xuan et al., 2021). In this context, attaining high sensitivity is a major issue for sweat glucose level quantification owing to its low concentration in sweat. Using transduction layers as immo­ bilized matrices is one of the most effective ways to improve the sensi­ tivity of electrochemical sensors(Wang et al., 2021). Scientific and technological breakthroughs in the development of nanomaterials have led to the development of a new generation of electrochemical bio­ sensors(Cho et al., 2020). Various carbon compounds have been inves­ tigated for electrochemical biosensor applications. Owing to their remarkable electrochemical performance, high chemical stability, and high surface area, carbon-based materials, such as carbon nanotubes (CNT)(Guo, 2013), reduced graphene oxide (rGO)(Barman et al., 2018), carbon nanofibers (CNF)(Sharifuzzaman et al., 2022), multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNT)(Zhang et al., 2020) have demonstrated considerable potential as transduction materials. However, the inherent properties of aggregation by strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, low uniformity, hydrophobicity, and insolubility hinder their potential application in the field of electrochemical biosensor sensing(Cho et al., 2018, 2020; Kavosi et al., 2014; Li et al., 2017; Zheng et al., 2010).
Furthermore, achieving a wide linear range response for lactate sensors is desirable also. The linear range of the lactate sensor could be extended to cover sweat levels by lowering the lactate diffusion coeffi­ cient through the encapsulation layer, switching to a different electron transfer system, or increasing the thickness of the encapsulation layer. Such an enhancement may occur at the expense of a signal magnitude reduction(Rathee et al., 2016; Yokus et al., 2020). PVC-based mem­ branes could serve as a good choice for incorporating other materials, such as DOS(Kisiel et al., 2020), a common plasticizer used in electro­ chemistry to produce compatible PVC-based membranes, ETH 500, is a lipophilic electrolyte for the reduction of electrical resistance while enhancing the hydrophobicity of the outer membrane(Liang et al., 2015), and TDMAC is used as an anion exchanger(Grygolowicz-Pawlak and Bakker, 2010).
Sweating is a dynamic process that involves fluctuating temperature (T) and pH during exercise and sports, and the trends and levels differ significantly between subjects(Jia et al., 2013; Wiorek et al., 2020). As a result of variations in enzyme activity, such fluctuations are likely to alter the responsiveness of wearable enzymatic biosensors(Wiorek et al., 2020). Therefore, it is of great importance to calibrate sensors based on pH and temperature variations for enzymatic wearable biosensor quantification. In this context, Javey et al. addressed T-change compensation(Gao et al., 2016), while Alexander et al. discussed T and pH variation correction for enzymatic glucose biosensor calibration (Wiorek et al., 2020), and revealed that there was no apparent link between perspiration and blood glucose, and that additional research is needed to determine the correlation link between sweat and blood metabolites (glucose and lactate).
Furthermore, laser-induced graphene (LIG)(Lin et al., 2014), with wrinkles, holes, flakes, and ruptures on the surface serve as extremely active sites that facilitate further modification with other materials (Yang et al., 2020). However, owing to the line gap of the laser beam, the designed 3D porous graphene flakes are rarely interconnected, resulting in nonuniform conductivity and increased fragility of the graphene structure(Zahed et al., 2020). Owing to its poor mechanical qualities and hydrophobic properties, producing bare 3D porous graphene is also challenging for practical sensing applications(Zahed et al., 2021). To address these issues, nanoparticles incorporating LIG can be an alter­ native to enhance the analytical performance.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with large pore volumes and high surface areas have been investigated for many years, particularly in gas storage and supercapacitor applications(Torad et al., 2014b). However, researchers have recently become interested in a few MOFs as potential sources of nanoporous carbon (NPC) materials, which can dramatically increase electrical conductivity and enhance electrocatalytic activity (Chaikittisilp et al., 2013). Various ZIFs (subfamily of MOFs), such as ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 can be used as precursors for NPC synthesis. However, for NPCs composed from a single MOFs precursor, the intended func­ tions are constrained(Torad et al., 2014b; Yang et al., 2017), because the nitrogen (N) content of NPC dramatically reduces during graphitization; hence, it is difficult to simultaneously achieve both a high nitrogen (N) content and highly graphitic structure. NPC derived from ZIF-8 is poorly conductive owing to the incorporation of more amorphous carbon but has a large surface area and high N content(Tang et al., 2015). Mean­ while, ZIF-67 derived NPCs exhibit high electrical conductivity owing to their highly graphitic structure because of the catalytic effect of cobalt nanoparticles, though ZIF-67 derived NPCs have a lower surface area than ZIF-8 derived NPC(Torad et al., 2014a). In this manner, hybrid core-shell MOFs (ZIF-8@ZIF-67) crystal precursors are considered for the synthesis of hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) for use as a transduction material to attain the large surface area of ZIF-8 and the high electrical conductivity of ZIF-67.
In this study, we successfully synthesized Core-shell structured, termed, hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) from core-shell ZIF${8@\mathrm{ZIF-67}}$ crystals and introduced a flexible, affordable, and multifunctional hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC)/LIG (3D graphene) -based second-generation electrochemical biosensor platform for the detection of lactate, glucose, temperature, and pH in human perspira­ tion. Fig. 1A Illustrates the synthesis procedure for the developed ma­ terial. To the best of our knowledge, hybridized nanoporous carbon (HNPC) has been used to modify the working electrodes of a sensor array for the first time in this work, which successfully overcomes the limi­ tations of bare LIG. Additionally, the incorporation of H-NPC nano­ particles into the porous holes and flakes of LIG strengthened flake interconnectivity and enhanced the electrochemical surface area, het­ erogeneous electron transfer rate, and electrocatalytic activity. For the lactate biosensor, an outer diffusion limiting layer (DLL) composed of a lipophilic salt, polymer, and plasticizer was used to limit the flux of lactate reaching the LOx enzyme. This outer DLL has been used to pre­ vent the enzyme-based electrode from coming into direct contact with the sample solution, thus reducing lactate diffusion and enzyme satu­ ration, which results in a wide linear range of lactate detection. All the sensor modification processes are schematically illustrated in Fig. 1B. Finally, based on sweat temperature (T) and $\mathsf{p H}$ variation, we adopted an analytical approach to adjust the glucose and lactate concentrations obtained by the glucose and lactate biosensors. Fig. 1C shows a sche­ matic of the developed wearable patch. The promising results obtained with the developed materials (H-NPC) in combination with the multi­ plexing sensing system represent another step toward an on-body solu­ tion for the continuous monitoring of metabolic indicators.
