## 3.1. Physical characterization of various modified LIG electrodes
Core–shell MOFs $(\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67)$ -derived hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) was synthesized using a seed-mediated growth tech­ nique. Owing to their isoreticular structures and similar unit cell pa­ rameters between ZIF-8 $\mathbf{\tilde{a}}=\mathbf{b}=\mathbf{c}=16.9910\$ (Park et al., 2006) and ZIF-67 $\mathbf{\tilde{a}}=\mathbf{b}=\mathbf{c}=16.9589)$ (Banerjee et al., 2008), ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 are possible candidates for the preparation of core-shell structures (ZIF-8@ZIF-67). The surface morphologies of LIG, ZIF-8/LIG, ZIF-67/LIG, core-shell ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67/LIG, Co/Zn-NPC, H-NPC/LIG, and PB/H-NPC/LIG were characterized using field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). Fig. 2(A-D) shows the rhombic dodeca­ hedral geometry of the synthesized ZIF-8 and ZIF-67, Co/Zn-NPC, and core-shell ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67 crystals on the LIG substrate. The FESEM im­ ages (Figs. S2a, S2b, and S2c) show the surface morphologies of bare LIG, H-NPC/LIG, and PB/H-NPC/LIG, respectively. The rhombic dodecahedral geometry of the precursors was well maintained by the core-shell ZIF-8@ZIF-67, as shown in the FESEM images in Fig. 2D. When the edge section with fractures was examined, it was discovered that the ZIF-67 shell uniformly coated the ZIF-8 core with a thickness of $95\mathrm{nm}$ , as illustrated in Fig. 2E, confirming the successful synthesis of the core-shell structure of ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystals. Fig. 2F shows transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of H-NPC that depict the well-retained rhombic dodecahedral-shaped surface morphology of the developed materials, and Fig. 2G illustrates the TEM images of the core-shell ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystal, while Fig. 2H-J shows the line scan and elemental mapping, which also verifies the successful formation of core-shell ZIF- ${8}@\mathrm{ZIF-}67$ crystals from their parents’ precursors, namely ZIF-8 (Core) and ZIF-67 (Shell) crystals.
X-ray Diffraction (XRD) was used to study the crystal structures of the synthesized ZIF-8, core-shell ZIF- $3@$ ZIF-67, Co/Zn-NPC, H-NPC, ZIF-8@ZIF-67/LIG, Co/Zn-NPC/LIG, and H-NPC/LIG. The results are presented in Fig. 2K. The dominant diffraction peaks for $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Zn}$ -NPC and H-NPC at approximately $2\theta=15^{\circ}$ and $26^{\circ}$ belong to the (001) and (002) facets of graphene oxide and graphite respectively(Siburian et al., 2018), confirming the effective conversion of core-shell ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystals to hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) with good graphiti­ zation. Other vibrant peaks located at approximately 38, 46, and 69 correspond to the crystal structures of the metals (Zn, Co, and Au). The XRD patterns of ZIF-8 and core-shell ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67 crystals helped clarify the core-shell crystals, as the positions of the diffraction peaks were identical.
Fig. 2L depicts the Raman spectra of ZIF-8@ZIF-67, Co/Zn-NPC, and H-NPC, highlighting the successful synthesis and atomic configuration of the hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) particles. The rhombic dodecahedral structure of ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67 was identified in its Raman spectrum which exhibited various low-intensity peaks at 243, 408, 677, 1339, and $2683~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Sundriyal et al., 2020). The D (defective carbon) and G (graphitic carbon) bands are responsible for the peaks at 1339 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1586\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ -NPC and H-NPC, respectively(Wang and Li, 2016). In this case, the D-peak formed by defects in the graphitic lattice of the disordered $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ hybridized carbon aided the incorporation of N heteroatoms(Zahed et al., 2020). The G to D bands comparative ratios $\mathrm{(I_{G}/I_{D})}$ are critical metrics for determining the degree of graphi­ tization. The $\mathrm{I_{G}/I_{D}}$ ratios of $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ -NPC and H-NPC were nearly the same (approximately 1), indicating the presence of the same number of graphitic crystallites and N-doping atoms at the nanoporous carbon borders, which could be beneficial for boosting the catalytic activity. The increased peak height of H-NPC compared with that of ${\mathrm{Co/Zn–NPC}}$ for both $\mathrm{I_{G}}$ and $\mathrm{I_{D}}$ peaks reveals the enhanced degree of graphitization due to the etching of Zn and Co atoms by hydrofluoric acid treatment (Salunkhe et al., 2016).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was employed to examine the chemical composition of hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC), as shown in Fig. 2M. The entire scan spectrum revealed the presence of Zn (as core), Co (as shell), O, C, and N, indicating the presence of core-shell nanoporous carbon nanoparticles retaining their parent elements. The graphitic structure of H-NPC was demonstrated by deconvolution of the C1s spectra (Fig. S3), which showed peaks at approximately 284.6, 285.8, 287.3, and 288.7 eV, corresponding to C–C, $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}_{:}$ , C–O, and C–N bonds, respectively(Li et al., 2019). In addition, Figs. S4–S5 illustrate the
N1s and Co2p deconvolution peaks of the H-NPC sample. Accordingly, the N1s spectrum can be deconvoluted into three primary peaks at 398.7 eV, 399.8 eV, and $404~\mathrm{eV}$ , which correspond to pyridinic N, graphitic N, and Oxidation-N, respectively(Zhang et al., 2021). The findings reveal that the amount of graphitic and pyridinic nitrogen in H-NPC is so rich that it improves their electrocatalytic activity and chemical stability(Karthikayini et al., 2016). Additionally, the $\mathtt{C o2p}$ peak was deconvoluted into three peaks, each corresponding to a different part of the spectrum with cobalt $(779.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ , oxidized cobalt $(781.5~\mathrm{eV})$ , and satellite cobalt (784.8 eV)(Karthikayini et al., 2016). Cobalt coordinated to nitrogen functional groups and cobalt oxides are likely to be present because the O1s peak can be observed in the survey scan. Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis of the synthesized core-shell hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) is shown in the Sup­ porting Information (Fig. S6), which illustrates the presence of all the desired elements with the corresponding atomic and weight percentages tabulated in Table S1. After detailed electrochemical characterization of various modified electrodes investigation (detailed in Supporting In­ formation Fig. S7.), H-NPC-based electrode is considered as optimized.
## 3.2. Individual characterization of the patch sensors
The electrochemical investigation of the developed glucose biosensor using various developed materials in artificial sweat $\mathrm{(pH=}$ 6.6) under ambient conditions is shown in Fig. 3A, in which the sensi­ tivities of all the electrodes are compared with the chronoamperometry responses of the biosensors manufactured using newly established ma­ terials for a range of glucose concentration from $0.3~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $1.5~\mathrm{mM}$ at $-0.05\mathrm{V}$ reduction potential. The H-NPC-based electrode had the highest current response, indicating enhanced catalytic activity of the biosensor. Furthermore, the increased surface area of H-NPC allows for a large amount of enzyme loading. Consequently, the optimal glucose-sensing electrode (LIG/H-NPC/PB/GOx) configuration was chosen and used for glucose detection in the physiological range of human sweat. Fig. 3B illustrates the chronoamperometry responses in artificial sweat $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}$ at $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for successive additions of glucose in a $0.3\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $1.5\mathrm{mM}$ range. The linear calibration curve ${\bf(n=3)}$ ) derived from the stepwise current response as a function of glucose quantity is depicted in Fig. 3C. To calculate the sensitivity of the biosensors, current values were consid­ ered at every 70th sec from the chronoamperometric responses. The glucose biosensor performed an outstanding sensitivity of 82.7 $\mathbf{\bar{\upmu}}\mathbf{AmM}^{-1}\mathbf{cm}^{-2}$ with an extraordinary correlation coefficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ value of 0.99972 and using the formula $\mathrm{LOD}=3.3~\mathrm{S_{b}}/\mathrm{S}$ (Mani et al., 2016) (where $s_{\mathrm{b}}$ indicates the residual standard deviation of the linear regression at low concentrations and S denotes the slope of the regression line), the limit of detection (LOD) was determined to be $0.025\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The selectivity test of the glucose biosensor in the presence of interfering molecules is presented in Fig. 3D with $0.3\mathrm{mM}$ , $0.6\mathrm{mM}$ , and $0.9\mathrm{mM}$ glucose injection. The sensor did not respond to any significant interfering compounds, indicating a strong glucose selectivity. Six glucose sensors were randomly chosen from a batch of samples and evaluated in three iterations under identical experimental conditions. As shown in Fig. 3E, the RSDs of the biosensors was $3.35\%$ toward $1\mathrm{mM}$ of glucose. Fig. 3F illustrates the results of the sensor under bending con­ ditions to assess the flexibility and stability of the glucose sensors, the test was performed in an artificial sweat solution containing $1~\mathrm{mM}$ of glucose under ambient circumstances which shows the chronoampero­ metric responses at different bending with RSD value of $3.19\%$ indicate the sensor’s mechanical resilience in extreme situations, such as heavy exercise. The results indicate that these sensors are highly reproducible, implying that they can be used interchangeably in the sensing platform. An optical image of the multimodal patch and bending images is shown in Fig. S8 in the Supporting Information. Table S3 presents a comparison of various state-of-the-art wearable glucose biosensors. The developed system outperformed previously reported carbon ink/PB, CNT/PB, $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{3}T_{x}/P B}$ , and graphene/PB-based biosensors.
A temperature sensor that conforms to the skin should be responsive to changes in skin temperature. The serpentine-shaped temperature sensor comprises a laser-induced graphene (LIG)/H-NPC layer whose resistance fluctuates with temperature. Because of the enhanced electron-phonon scattering and thermal velocity of electrons in the sandwiched layers, the LIG provides unique properties for building resistive physical sensors, and as the temperature rises, its conductivity increases(Shao et al., 2008; Yang et al., 2020). Fig. 3G depicts the dy­ namic resistance responses of the temperature sensor after applying incremental temperatures from a constant temperature controller that revealed the resistance value in decreasing order with an increasing temperature, thereby showing a negative temperature coefficient of resistance (NTCR). This is obviously owing to the semiconductive nature of MOFs(Panchariya et al., 2018) and the enhanced electron-phonon scattering and thermal velocity of electrons in the LIG(Yang et al., 2020). Fig. 3H shows the linear fitting curve between the temperature and resistance for the temperature sensor. With a sensitivity of $-0.053\%$ $\mathsf{C}^{-1}$ as illustrated in Fig. 3I, and an excellent correlation coefficient $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$
of 0.999, the temperature sensor responded to temperature changes quickly, accurately, and consistently.
Fig. 4A illustrates the diffusion limiting layer (DLL) based lactate biosensor chronoamperometry responses for successive addition in the range of $1\mathrm{mM-}56\mathrm{mM}$ of lactate and the linear calibration curve derived from the stepwise current response as a function of lactate quantity is shown in Fig. 4B. With a correlation coefficient $(\mathrm{R}^{2})$ of 0.9937, the lactate biosensor demonstrated an excellent sensitivity of 204 $\mathrm{\nAmM^{-1}c m^{-2}}$ , particularly with a wide range of concentrations of lactate. The LOD was calculated to be $4~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . However, Nafion membrane-based lactate sensors (using Nafion as a permiselective membrane) showed only a dynamic linearity range of up to $21~\mathrm{mM}$ lactate concentration, as shown in Fig. 4C. A consistent linear response does not exist beyond $12~\mathrm{mM}$ of lactate injection because the enzyme becomes saturated beyond $12~\mathrm{mM}$ lactate concentration, though the overall high sensitivity of $5.06~{\upmu\mathrm{AmM}^{-1}}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was calculated using the linear fitting curve shown in Fig. 4D. Therefore, in this study, we present a new lactate biosensor using the developed transducing material (HNPC) with an extraordinary linear range (up to $56~\mathrm{mM}$ and compara­ tively very high sensitivity owing to the high electrocatalytic properties of the developed material (H-NPC) in sweat using an outer membrane composed of (PVC/DOS/ETH500), a polymer, lipophilic salt, and plas­ ticizer to limit the flux of lactate reaching the LOx enzyme. This enhanced linear range and sensitivity are due to the large surface area responsible for high enzyme loading, high electrocatalytic performance, and good hydrophilic properties of the developed hybrid nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) materials. Fig. 4E illustrates the selectivity test of the lactate biosensor in the presence of interfering molecules with $2\mathrm{mM}$ , 4 mM, and $6~\mathrm{mM}$ lactate injection. No significant interfering chemicals were detected by the sensor, indicating high lactate selectivity. Six lactate sensors were randomly selected from each batch of samples and tested in three iterations under the same experimental settings. The RSD of the lactate biosensors toward $15\mathrm{mM}$ lactate was $1.63\%$ , as shown in Fig. 4F. A comparison with various state-of-the-art Prussian blue (PB)- based lactate sensors is given in Table S4.
The pH sensor was tested in commercial standard buffer solutions with values ranging from 3 to 9, and it exhibited (Fig. 4G) outstanding open circuit potential (OCP) responses over multiple repetitions (2 it­ erations). An electrochemical workstation (CHI660E) was employed to record the OCPs after the pH sensor was introduced into the buffer so­ lutions. We recorded the OCPs for $50~s$ at each pH and then paused the test to transition to a buffer solution with different pH values. During consecutive readings, there was no significant difference in the specific pH levels, indicating excellent reversibility of the pH sensor. As shown in Fig. 4H, the sensor exhibited a good linear response with an excellent sensitivity of $-64.086~\mathrm{mV/pH}$ and a high regression coefficient value $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9994)$ . In other words, the proposed sensor can detect changes in pH within the typical $\mathtt{p H}$ range of human perspiration(Yoon et al., 2022). The sensitivity calculated from the linear regression curve exceeded the Nernst limit of pH sensitivity. We employed an electrically conductive polyaniline polymer as a $\mathtt{p H}$ sensor in our flexible sensor array, the protonation of which is highly influenced by the oxidation state of the polymer and the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the aqueous solution(Yokus et al., 2020). The sensing capabilities and conductivity of PANI depend on its deposition technique and phases. In this study, the Nernst limit of the pH sensor was exceeded, possibly because of the superior orientation of PANI achieved via electrodeposition on the H-NPC/LIG electrode(Oh et al., 2018). With increasing number of deposited segments, the anodic and cathodic peak currents successively increased (Fig. S7E) during electropolymerization, ensuring the effective deposition of PANI. The sensitivity of the sensor was improved owing to the increased electronic conductivity and surface area of the H-NPC and LIG composites. From the same batch of $\mathsf{p H}$ sensors, three electrodes were randomly chosen and tested against buffer solutions of pH 3, 5, and 7 under the identical experimental conditions, where the RSD for the electrode-to-electrode for repeatability of four ${\bf\tilde{n}}=4$ , n is the number of tests) was $2.2\%$ , as shown in Fig. 4I. Furthermore, because body fluids contain a range of electrolytes, such as $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , and $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , it is important to examine how these major electrolytes affect sensor function. The $\mathfrak{p H}$ sensor exhibited good selectivity (Fig. 4J) for pH (7 and 5), without noticeable responses for other ions $\mathrm{{(Na^{+}}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , and $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+})$ ). The relative responses for thirty consecutive measurements in a pH (7) buffer solution using an identical pH sensor are displayed in Fig. 4K. The OCP responses for the 5th, 10th, 15th, and 20th tests are depicted in the inset Fig., which indicates the good repeatability performance of the pH sensor. Furthermore, Fig. 4L shows that the pH sensor is stable over time and can be used several times. The RSD of the pH sensor toward the buffer solution $(\mathrm{pH}=7)$ ) was $1.36\%$ for 7 days of testing with error bars for three separate sensors, demonstrating the long-term durability of the sensor. A comparison with other state-of-the-art PANI-based pH sensors
is presented in Table S5.
## 3.3. Evaluation of the patch for on-body perspiration analysis
Since changes in sample pH and temperature (T) are likely to affect the performance of enzymatic biosensors, glucose and lactate biosensor calibrations were tested under various pH and temperature (T) condi­ tions. Sweat has a $\mathsf{p H}$ of 4–7.5, depending on the subject, and the tem­ perature can range from $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $38^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ depending on the body part(Chen et al., 2017). Fig. 5A depicts the responses of the glucose biosensor to increasing temperatures in artificial sweat at pH (6.6) and the chro­ noamperometric responses of the glucose biosensor at various pH values of artificial sweat are presented in Fig. 5B. Similarly, the chro­ noamperometric responses of the lactate biosensor at varying tempera­ tures and pH values are shown in Fig. 5C-D. Evidently, the chronoamperometric signal enhanced as the temperature was raised, which is attributable to heightened enzyme activity. However, the cur­ rent responses were significantly reduced above $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S9), which could be related to a disruption in the enzyme environment and intrinsic metallic characteristics of the transduction materials. The pH levels of the artificial sweat were adjusted by mixing with diluted HCl and NaOH solutions. A constant temperature controller and testing beaker with inlet and exit valves were employed to maintain a stable temperature. The corresponding calibration curves at varying temperatures and pH values for the glucose sensor are illustrated in Fig. S10(A-B) in the Supporting Information, and the corresponding calibration linear fitting curves of the lactate sensors are shown in Fig. S11(A-B). The slope of the glucose and lactate biosensor responses decreased as the pH of the artificial sweat decreased, indicating a clear dependence on the pH value. In theory, this is a direct result of the decrease in enzyme activity with decreasing pH(Prieto et al., 2015).
If the pH and temperature (T) in sweat drop significantly, the biosensor loses sensitivity, and the intercept shifts to higher negative currents in both cases. The trends observed for both the T and pH effects will be used to develop a correction for the chronoamperometric response of the biosensor. The glucose and lactate concentrations were recalculated using the slope $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ and intercept (c) of the off-body cali­ bration equations (from Figs. 3C and 4B) in artificial sweat at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ and $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for reference, given the cumulative effects of pH and temperature (T) on the glucose and lactate biosensor. Monitoring the perspiration after intensive stationary cycling validated the viability of the chemical and temperature sensors of the patch. The multi-modal patch worn on the chest of the cyclist is presented in Fig. 5E. After $20\mathrm{min}$ of riding, the sweat-absorbing layer collected sufficient perspiration for analyte quantification. An enlarged image of the patch affixed to the chest is shown in Fig. 5F. Before the meal, immediately after the meal, and $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ after the meal, the participant sweated under three distinct conditions for glucose sensor measurement. However, in case of the lactate sensor, chronoamperometric responses were taken at $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $30~\mathrm{min}$ , and 40 min during exercise for lactate quantification as lactate concentration varied with intense exercise. Fig. 5G shows the cycling-resistance profile over time. The chronoamperometric responses obtained directly from the multi-functional patch affixed to the chest are depicted in Fig. 5H, M for the glucose and lactate sensors. From Figs. 3C and 4B, linear equa­ tions were considered as a reference and used to compute the corre­ sponding glucose and lactate concentrations, which are then shown in Fig. 5I, O, respectively, and regarded as sweat glucose and lactate before pH and T correction. After eating, the glucose concentration during perspiration increased significantly and then progressively decreased over time. Meanwhile, the temperature and pH of sweat fluctuate based on the duration, intensity of exercise, amount of food consumed, and enzyme activity, which can increase or decrease as a result of these factors. For each test event, the sweat pH and temperature were measured using the same patch and compared to values obtained using a commercially available pH meter and thermometer. By comparing the results with commercial devices, Fig. 5J and K demonstrate the usability of the fabricated pH and temperature (T) sensors. Therefore, we manually calculated the corrected slopes $(m_{c o r r e c t e d})$ and intercepts (ccorrected), which were later used to derive accurate glucose and lactate concentrations during perspiration during the on-body test using a specific correction algorithm. In the Supporting Information, the cali­ bration process for precise glucose (S.4.), and lactate (S.5.) concentra­ tion determination based on pH and T fluctuations is detailed. A commercial glucometer was employed to measure blood glucose levels of the subject before each test event. Between the glucose levels in sweat and blood, there was a time lag of approximately $20\mathrm{min}$ (Klonoff, 1997). As a result, approximately $20\mathrm{min}$ after blood glucose was detected by a commercial glucometer, sweat glucose measured by the multifunctional patch was obtained. In contrast, sweat glucose levels with blood glucose levels for different states of exercise after pH and T correction obtained by the fabricated patch are illustrated in Fig. 5L. Following the same analytical procedure for the glucose sensor correction mechanism, the lactate sensor response was corrected, and the calculated and obtained values are depicted in Fig. 5O. The glucose concentration in sweat increased significantly after consuming a meal and then progressively decreased over time, similar to the behavior of glucose in the blood. Furthermore, owing to human homeostasis(Rosen and Spiegelman, 2006), the response for the ‘2h after meal’ samples decreased with time. Beyond the same qualitative trend, no clear correlation was observed between perspiration and blood glucose level. Overall, further research is required to fully understand this correlation. For the lactate sensor, the concentration of lactate in the sweat increased as the cycling resis­ tance increased (after $30~\mathrm{{min}}.$ ), implying a link between physical exer­ cise and lactate generation following a physiological delay. The third stage of the cycling took a 5 min cool-down period, during which the lactate concentration detected by the biosensor decreased. This drop was caused by the sweat dilution factor and a reduction in riding in­ tensity. When the chronoamperometric responses of the multi-functional patch for glucose and lactate were calibrated using changes in $\mathsf{p H}$ and sweat temperature (T) during real-time measure­ ments, more accurate results were obtained.
## 4. Conclusion
For the persistent and sensitive detection of sweat biomarkers, a laser-induced graphene (LIG)-based flexible multifunctional patch incorporating hybridized nanoporous carbon (H-NPC) was newly developed. The fabricated PB/H-NPC/LIG electrodes exhibited excellent electrochemical activity and stability over physiological sweat glucose and wide lactate concentration ranges. The pH and temperature sensors were also highly effective to reflect changes in local pH values and skin temperature, respectively. More precise glucose and lactate concentra­ tions were observed when the biosensor response was successfully calibrated with changes in sweat pH and temperature based on the intake of meals and exercise. We believe that this work is a crucial step towards the development of highly sensitive enzymatic multifunctional biosensing patch for the early identification of diseases and personalized healthcare. In near future, the developed epidermal patch platform will be integrated with a flexible circuit PCB to develop a compact, minia­ turized, and wearable smart health surveillance system.
